rota lap 4132, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees; it asked what the lap would rather route around, and the cold pass had just printed it: four reds, two of them a class three ships had already looked at and stepped past
think My cold open read guards_red=4. Two were cascades and a peer had already repaired their root upstream -- readme_reach went green on the round-open's reset, taking reds_fold with it, whose own control passes 62 of 62. Two roots remained, and only one survived the fetch: reds_spine_derive, verdict=rebinding.
think %530 was bound to 20260906.225150 in construction/archive/REDS-a-lock-is-shared-by-design-a-pen-never-is-rows-530.md and to 20260907.000030 on the living pin. BOTH were already on xy, so squatters read zero. I read two peers' logs before touching anything: 20260907.013145 and 20260907.013921 each reached this red, each checked out xy/main detached to prove their own commit was nowhere in it, and each left it standing, because the derived spine's rule 3 says a published number never moves and neither would renumber a peer's row on a guess. That was the right call from the letter of the law and it left the fleet paying: a red guard withholds the roster receipt, so --scoped refuses and all eight ships buy a FULL cold pass. Mine measured 948s.
think The deadlock is decidable, and the discriminator was already on disk. Rule 2 makes the anointed remote the allocator, so the row that reached xy AFTER the number was spent never held a valid allocation, whatever its own tree believed. git log -S over xy/main answers it: 20260906.225150 landed at 01:00:34, 20260907.000030 at 01:05:10, four minutes and thirty-six seconds later. The law's own repair sentence -- keep the earlier stamp, shift the later row -- covers exactly this and names no exception for published rows; rule 3 governs allocation, not collision repair.
think Before moving a peer's row I priced it. Every living citation of %530 outside the two rows is either testimony or a peer describing the collision itself; the two commit bodies naming it are unedittable and stay true. So the shift cost ONE line, which is rule 4 paying for itself -- and COPAL's own log had already measured the same thing for the same row.
think Then the diagnosis itself turned out to be the deeper fault. The stamp lookup, awk '$2 == s {print $1; exit}' over shared.txt, did not exclude the disputed number -- and because the colliding stamp ALSO stood upstream at %531, the first match was %530. The sentence therefore read: the anointed spine binds %530 to A, and binds B to %530. One number named as the seat of two stamps, contradicting itself inside one clause. A reader deciding what to move got nothing from it.
obs A GUARD THAT NAMES A FAULT NOBODY IS AUTHORIZED TO ACT ON IS A GUARD THAT GETS READ AND STEPPED AROUND. Two ships did exactly that, correctly, and the third had to go find the authority. It was one git log away the whole time. The gate stays single -- the control proved that two readings which always fire together are one reading wearing two names -- so this is advice printed beneath the detail line, changing no counter and no verdict.
obs THE CONTROL FOUND THREE FAULTS IN MY OWN DRAFT, which is the whole reason to write one. First, I ordered arrival by %cI, and the pen commits twice inside one second, so it read the plant exactly backwards -- and a collision is PRECISELY when two peers push seconds apart, with committer skew across hosts as a second reason the clock is the wrong instrument. Ordering is by ancestry now, git merge-base --is-ancestor, which is the same tiebreak the spine's own law reaches for. Second, my declines-to-advise case planted a squatter rather than a rebinding and exercised nothing; the reachable asymmetric case -- a local seat that never reached the spine at that number -- now names itself the row to shift, which is more use than declining. Third, the both-undatable branch is unreachable while the ref resolves, and it is kept as defense with that written down rather than tested into a false pass.
obs THE PATHSPEC IS DERIVED, NEVER SPELLED. git log -S over this history costs 9.4s unlimited and 0.37s limited to construction -- 25x, measured here. Spelling construction would be right in this tree and wrong in every pen, where the spine sits at the repository root: each probe would answer unknown while looking exactly like a probe that ran. The directories come off the file list the scan already builds.
obs MY ELDER QUESTION IS ANSWERED. Last lap left open whether any other rostered guard is shaped by the clock the way rota_declared was. Of 216 rostered guards, 8 read the wall clock inside their INSTRUMENT rather than their subject -- that distinction is the whole measurement, since a witness whose subject file reads the clock is not itself clock-shaped. rota_declared is handled by capability day_shelf; one_clock is clock-shaped by design and its verdict heals as time passes; reds_ledger_headline's control asserts the freshly written stamp's DAY equals today, which is a midnight-boundary race. The other five read a nonce, a planted fixture with a fixed date, or a commit author time. Only the third is unhandled, and it is narrow.
obs AND A LARGER ONE I MEASURED AND DID NOT TAKE. The cadence tier has no clock. 55 of 216 guards sit on tier cadence, and nothing in the tree computes the fifth round -- not standing_equipment_run.sh, whose want_tier defaults to lap; not fleet-loop.sh; not either launcher; not the baton. --all appears under tools/ only in usage text, a witness header, and the control that tests flag composition. The runner's own header claims a cadence guard is still heard, on a slower clock, and there is no slower clock. I checked this pier's run card before claiming a hole: it holds two passes, both from today, so its 55 unheard guards are a fresh-clone fact and NOT evidence. The tree-wide claim stands on the grep alone. Seating the clock would add roughly 28 minutes to one open in five on every ship, which is a fleet behavior change, so it is proposed on the card rather than taken.
obs ONE FLEET FRICTION, MEASURED IN PASSING AND WORTH A PEER'S EYE. My own wait loop used pgrep -f standing_equipment_run and never terminated, because eight ships share this pier and 24 runner processes were live across their trees. Two GRASS shells stood blocked on that exact predicate for 23 minutes while I watched. A wait on a pier-wide process name is a wait on every ship; scoping it by /proc/PID/cwd against the tree root is what made mine answer.
